.TH TUNNY 1 "2026-08-12" "tunny" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
tunny \- open an admin SSH tunnel chosen from a list

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tunny
.RI [ name ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-retry ]
.br
.B tunny \-\-preview
.RI [ width ]
.br
.B tunny \-\-version

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tunny
opens an
.BR ssh (1)
port forward that you pick from a list rather than remember the name of, and
brings it back when the network drops.
.PP
With no argument it draws a picker: type to filter on the name and the
description at once, arrows to move, Enter to open, Escape to give up. The
destination you used last is on top. With a name, it goes straight there and
never draws anything.
.PP
The tunnel runs in the foreground and Ctrl\-C closes it.
.B tunny
never reimplements SSH: it builds an argument list and executes the real
.BR ssh (1)
binary, so aliases, ProxyJump, the agent, known_hosts and the host\-key prompt
all keep working the way you configured them.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-no\-retry
One attempt. Do not relaunch if the tunnel drops.
.TP
.BI \-\-preview " width"
Print what the picker would show and exit, without opening anything or writing
any state. An optional width, at least 20, draws the layout at a size other
than this terminal's.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version on standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary on standard error and exit.

.SH RECONNECTION
Three attempts per outage, waiting 1, 2 and 4 seconds.
.PP
A tunnel that held for thirty seconds resets the counter, so the count is per
outage rather than per session: one left open all day survives five network
changes, while a destination that is genuinely down gives up in three quick
tries.
.PP
Ctrl\-C never relaunches.
.B tunny
catches the interrupt itself rather than reading ssh's exit code, and takes the
ssh child down with it on SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP alike.
.PP
Two failures skip the retries and hand you ssh's own words, since both would
fail identically three times over:
.I Permission denied
and
.IR "Could not resolve hostname" .
A third, a local port already taken, never reaches ssh at all:
.B tunny
binds the port first, marks the destination in the picker, and refuses at
launch with the command that names what holds it.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tunny/destinations.toml
The destinations. Honours
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
See
.B EXAMPLE
below.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tunny/recent
The recency order, one name per line, most recent first, mode 0600. Honours
.BR XDG_STATE_HOME .
Names no longer in the config are dropped when it is read.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to any value, empty included, switches colour off.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
Anything but
.I 0
keeps colour through a pipe.
.B NO_COLOR
still wins over it.
.TP
.B TERM
.I dumb
switches colour off.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The tunnel was closed on purpose. Also Escape in the picker, and
.B \-\-version
and
.BR \-\-preview .
.TP
.B 1
Anything that failed: no config, unknown destination, local port taken, ssh
gave up.
.TP
.B 2
An unrecognised flag.

.SH EXAMPLE
.PP
A destination is one ssh invocation, with as many forwards as there are UIs
behind it:
.PP
.EX
[[destination]]
name = "hypervisor"
desc = "Cockpit and the orchestrator"
host = "my-host"
forward = [
  { local = 9090, to = "127.0.0.1:9090", label = "Cockpit" },
  { local = 9120, to = "10.0.0.5:9120", label = "Komodo" },
]
.EE
.PP
.I host
is an alias from
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
which already carries the user, the port and the key. The optional
.I port
and
.I jump
fields become
.B \-p
and
.BR \-J ,
for what the alias does not cover.
.PP
.B tunny
validates the file before it does anything: unique names, at least one forward,
ports in range, a host:port on the far side. A key it does not know is an error
too, since
.I forwards
instead of
.I forward
would otherwise leave a destination with no tunnel and no complaint.

.SH REQUIREMENTS
An OpenSSH client on
.BR PATH .
.B tunny
checks for it before printing anything.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR lsof (8)
.PP
